   "hypothesis_id": "H1",
   "mechanism": "GWAS firmly establish MEIS1 and BTBD9 as major risk genes for Restless Legs Syndrome. In mouse models, disruption of either gene results in profound sleep/wake cycle abnormalities and altered sleep behavior. Crucially, loss of MEIS1 leads to a disinhibition of cerebellar output, and loss of BTBD9 results in abnormal morphology of Purkinje cells, the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex. These converging phenotypes suggest that MEIS1 and BTBD9 risk variants disrupt normal Purkinje cell function and cerebellar output, driving the involuntary motor manifestations and sleep disturbances of the disorder.",
   "support": "speculative",
   "supporting_evidence": [],
   "title": "MEIS1 and BTBD9 risk variants disrupt cerebellar Purkinje cell morphology and output, driving motor and sleep phenotypes in Restless Legs Syndrome."
  },
