   "hypothesis_id": "H2",
   "mechanism": "PTPRD is a membrane-bound phosphatase that plays a critical role in bidirectionally inducing pre- and post-synaptic differentiation in neurons. In animal models, loss of the PTPRD orthologue causes significant motor impairments, including decreased grip strength and impaired swimming. The significant GWAS risk variants in PTPRD associated with Restless Legs Syndrome likely confer a loss of function that impairs synaptic connectivity in descending motor pathways, thereby contributing to the abnormal motor symptoms characteristic of the disease.",
   "support": "speculative",
   "supporting_evidence": [],
   "title": "PTPRD loss of function impairs synaptic differentiation and drives motor deficits in Restless Legs Syndrome."
